window.SEARCH=[{"t": "1. Data Ingestion & Chunking", "u": "ingestion.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "1.1 Fixed-size chunking that ignores document structure", "u": "ingestion.html#fixed-size-chunking", "s": "Ingestion & Chunking", "k": "Quality Chunking is a modelling decision  not a preprocessing default  Chunk along document structure  carry the heading path into every chunk  and prove the choice with a retrieval eval "}, {"t": "1.2 No metadata or lineage on chunks", "u": "ingestion.html#no-metadata-lineage", "s": "Ingestion & Chunking", "k": "Compliance Quality If you can t answer  where did this answer come from  which version  and who approved it   the system isn t production ready for a regulated client "}, {"t": "1.3 Parsing PDFs, tables and scans as flat text", "u": "ingestion.html#flat-pdf-tables", "s": "Ingestion & Chunking", "k": "Quality In document heavy industries the parser is the most underrated component  Bad parsing produces confident wrong numbers that no prompt can fix "}, {"t": "1.4 One-time ingestion with no incremental refresh or deletes", "u": "ingestion.html#one-time-ingestion", "s": "Ingestion & Chunking", "k": "Reliability Compliance A RAG index is a replica of the enterprise s knowledge  Treat it like any replica  with change data capture  delete propagation and a freshness SLA "}, {"t": "1.5 Ingesting PII and secrets without redaction", "u": "ingestion.html#pii-ingestion", "s": "Ingestion & Chunking", "k": "Security Compliance Data protection has to happen before embedding  Once PII is in the vector store  the logs and the caches  it can no longer be cleaned up reliably "}, {"t": "2. Embeddings & Vector Stores", "u": "embeddings.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "2.1 One embedding model for every domain and language", "u": "embeddings.html#one-embedding-model", "s": "Embeddings & Vector Stores", "k": "Quality Choose embeddings the way you d choose a database index  by measuring them on the real workload  A leaderboard tells you nothing about the client s part numbers "}, {"t": "2.2 No versioning when the embedding model changes", "u": "embeddings.html#no-embedding-versioning", "s": "Embeddings & Vector Stores", "k": "Reliability Cost Vectors are only comparable inside one model s space  Version the embedder with the index  migrate blue green  and let the eval decide promotion "}, {"t": "2.3 Using the vector database as the system of record", "u": "embeddings.html#vectordb-source-of-truth", "s": "Embeddings & Vector Stores", "k": "Reliability Compliance The vector index should be the cheapest thing to throw away  Keep raw and processed knowledge in governed storage and treat indexes as derived views "}, {"t": "2.4 No access control at retrieval time", "u": "embeddings.html#no-retrieval-acl", "s": "Embeddings & Vector Stores", "k": "Security Compliance Never ask the model to keep secrets  Authorization belongs in the retrieval layer  enforced with the user s own identity before anything enters the prompt "}, {"t": "3. Retrieval & RAG Core", "u": "rag-core.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "3.1 Naive top-k vector search with no hybrid retrieval", "u": "rag-core.html#naive-topk", "s": "Retrieval & RAG Core", "k": "Quality Hybrid retrieval is the production baseline  Dense only search fails on precisely the identifiers enterprise users care about most "}, {"t": "3.2 No reranking stage", "u": "rag-core.html#no-reranking", "s": "Retrieval & RAG Core", "k": "Quality Cost Retrieve wide and cheap  rerank narrow and precise  A reranker is usually the highest ROI single upgrade to a RAG system "}, {"t": "3.3 Stuffing the context window (\"lost in the middle\")", "u": "rag-core.html#context-stuffing", "s": "Retrieval & RAG Core", "k": "Quality Cost Latency Context is a budget  not a bucket  Long windows are great for whole document tasks  but retrieval plus ordering plus caching beats stuffing on cost  latency and accuracy "}, {"t": "3.4 No query rewriting or decomposition for multi-hop questions", "u": "rag-core.html#no-query-rewriting", "s": "Retrieval & RAG Core", "k": "Quality Users don t write search queries  they ask compound questions in conversation  Rewrite  decompose and route before you retrieve "}, {"t": "3.5 Answering when retrieval returns nothing relevant", "u": "rag-core.html#answering-without-evidence", "s": "Retrieval & RAG Core", "k": "Quality Compliance  I don t know  here s who does  is a feature  Design and measure abstention instead of letting the model improvise "}, {"t": "4. Advanced RAG", "u": "advanced-rag.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "4.1 GraphRAG or agentic RAG where simple RAG works", "u": "advanced-rag.html#overengineered-rag", "s": "Advanced RAG", "k": "Cost Latency Reliability Advanced RAG is a tool  not a maturity level  Profile the queries  default to simple  and escalate only for the segments that measurably need it "}, {"t": "4.2 No citation or grounding verification", "u": "advanced-rag.html#no-citation-verification", "s": "Advanced RAG", "k": "Quality Compliance A citation nobody verified is decoration  Make citations structured  check them claim by claim  and measure faithfulness "}, {"t": "4.3 Ignoring temporal freshness and document validity", "u": "advanced-rag.html#temporal-blindness", "s": "Advanced RAG", "k": "Quality Compliance  Correct  is time dependent  Index validity dates  resolve the question s time frame  and filter before ranking "}, {"t": "4.4 Using RAG to fix behaviour, or fine-tuning to add knowledge", "u": "advanced-rag.html#rag-vs-finetune-confusion", "s": "Advanced RAG", "k": "Cost Quality RAG and tools for knowledge  prompting or fine tuning for behaviour  Mixing them up produces stale facts or bloated prompts "}, {"t": "5. Prompt Engineering & Management", "u": "prompts.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "5.1 Prompts hardcoded in application code with no versioning", "u": "prompts.html#hardcoded-prompts", "s": "Prompt Engineering", "k": "Reliability Quality If a prompt can change a business decision  it needs version control  an eval gate and a trace ID  just like code "}, {"t": "5.2 The mega-prompt that does everything", "u": "prompts.html#mega-prompt", "s": "Prompt Engineering", "k": "Quality Cost Reliability One prompt per job  composed by code  Mega prompts are the monoliths of LLM applications "}, {"t": "5.3 Parsing free-text output with regex", "u": "prompts.html#freetext-parsing", "s": "Prompt Engineering", "k": "Reliability Don t parse prose  Ask for typed output with native structured output modes  then validate the business rules as well as the schema "}, {"t": "5.4 Relying on prompt instructions alone for safety and policy", "u": "prompts.html#prompt-only-safety", "s": "Prompt Engineering", "k": "Security Compliance A system prompt is a request  not a control  Safety needs layered  deterministic guards with logs that an auditor can test "}, {"t": "6. Model Selection & Routing", "u": "models.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "6.1 One frontier model for every task", "u": "models.html#one-model-for-all", "s": "Model Selection & Routing", "k": "Cost Latency Treat models like a portfolio  Route each task to the cheapest model that passes its eval  and escalate only when needed "}, {"t": "6.2 No fallback or multi-provider strategy", "u": "models.html#single-provider-no-fallback", "s": "Model Selection & Routing", "k": "Reliability Models are external dependencies with SLAs  Engineer them like payment gateways  with failover  circuit breakers and a degraded mode "}, {"t": "6.3 Fine-tuning too early", "u": "models.html#premature-finetuning", "s": "Model Selection & Routing", "k": "Cost Quality Fine tuning is the last rung of the ladder  not the first  Exhaust prompting  examples and retrieval against a baseline before you buy a model lifecycle "}, {"t": "6.4 Ignoring latency budgets and serving modes", "u": "models.html#ignoring-latency-budget", "s": "Model Selection & Routing", "k": "Latency Cost Latency is a design constraint  not a tuning afterthought  Budget it per step  stream  parallelize  cache  and batch whatever isn t interactive "}, {"t": "7. Single-Agent Design & Tool Use", "u": "agents.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "7.1 Building an agent where a deterministic workflow would do", "u": "agents.html#agent-for-workflow", "s": "Agent Design & Tool Use", "k": "Reliability Cost Start with the least autonomy that solves the problem  Agents are for unknown paths  not for running a process you already know "}, {"t": "7.2 Too many tools with vague descriptions", "u": "agents.html#tool-sprawl", "s": "Agent Design & Tool Use", "k": "Quality Cost Tools are a model facing API  Curate them  describe them like documentation  constrain their schemas  and load them on demand "}, {"t": "7.3 Tools with no input validation, idempotency or blast-radius limits", "u": "agents.html#unvalidated-tools", "s": "Agent Design & Tool Use", "k": "Security Reliability Treat every model generated argument as untrusted input  Validate it  bound it  make it idempotent  and require approval above a risk threshold "}, {"t": "7.4 Unbounded loops with no step, time or cost budget", "u": "agents.html#unbounded-loops", "s": "Agent Design & Tool Use", "k": "Cost Reliability An agent without a budget is an open ended cost commitment  Cap steps  tokens  time and money  detect stalls  and exit gracefully "}, {"t": "7.5 No state persistence or checkpointing", "u": "agents.html#no-checkpointing", "s": "Agent Design & Tool Use", "k": "Reliability Long running agents are workflows  Give them durable state  interrupts for humans  and replayable history "}, {"t": "8. Multi-Agent Systems", "u": "multi-agent.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "8.1 Multi-agent for its own sake (\"the org chart in code\")", "u": "multi-agent.html#org-chart-agents", "s": "Multi-Agent Systems", "k": "Cost Reliability Agents aren t headcount  Add one only for parallelism  context isolation or privilege separation  and prove it beats the single agent baseline "}, {"t": "8.2 Chatty agents passing full transcripts", "u": "multi-agent.html#chatty-agents", "s": "Multi-Agent Systems", "k": "Cost Latency Agents should exchange contracts  not conversations  Use typed hand offs  pass artifacts by reference  and throw away scratch work "}, {"t": "8.3 No supervisor, contract or termination condition", "u": "multi-agent.html#no-supervisor", "s": "Multi-Agent Systems", "k": "Reliability Every multi agent system needs an owner of  done   a supervisor  acceptance criteria and a revision limit "}, {"t": "8.4 Shared mutable memory without concurrency control", "u": "multi-agent.html#shared-mutable-memory", "s": "Multi-Agent Systems", "k": "Reliability Shared agent memory is a concurrency problem  Scope it  append rather than overwrite  version mutable records  and attribute every write "}, {"t": "8.5 Cascading failures with no circuit breakers", "u": "multi-agent.html#cascading-failures", "s": "Multi-Agent Systems", "k": "Reliability Cost Agent pipelines need the resilience patterns of distributed systems  contract checks  circuit breakers and staged writes "}, {"t": "9. Memory & Context Engineering", "u": "memory.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "9.1 Dumping the full chat history into every call", "u": "memory.html#full-history-dump", "s": "Memory & Context", "k": "Cost Quality Latency Context is assembled  not accumulated  Pin the essentials  keep recent turns  compact the rest  and retrieve on demand "}, {"t": "9.2 No separation of short-term, long-term and procedural memory", "u": "memory.html#undifferentiated-memory", "s": "Memory & Context", "k": "Quality Compliance Memory isn t one thing  Type it  scope it and give each type its own lifetime and trust level "}, {"t": "9.3 Unverified memory writes (memory poisoning)", "u": "memory.html#memory-poisoning", "s": "Memory & Context", "k": "Security Quality Memory can inform an agent but never authorize it  Verify  scope and expire memory writes  and keep permissions in systems of record "}, {"t": "9.4 No forgetting or retention policy", "u": "memory.html#no-forgetting", "s": "Memory & Context", "k": "Compliance Every copy of personal data in the AI stack counts  Inventory the stores  key them by data subject  and automate retention and erasure "}, {"t": "10. Protocols & Interoperability", "u": "protocols.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "10.1 Custom tool integrations per agent instead of a shared protocol", "u": "protocols.html#bespoke-integrations", "s": "Protocols (MCP & A2A)", "k": "Cost Scale Integrate each system once as an MCP server  govern access at a gateway  and let every agent reuse it "}, {"t": "10.2 MCP servers and A2A endpoints without authentication or trust boundaries", "u": "protocols.html#unauthenticated-mcp", "s": "Protocols (MCP & A2A)", "k": "Security MCP and A2A make integration easy  including for attackers  Allow list  pin  authenticate  sandbox  and treat tool metadata as untrusted input "}, {"t": "10.3 Over-privileged service accounts for agents", "u": "protocols.html#overprivileged-agents", "s": "Protocols (MCP & A2A)", "k": "Security Compliance An agent should never be more powerful than the person it serves  Give agents their own identity  delegate with OBO  and keep scopes narrow and short lived "}, {"t": "11. Evaluation, Observability & LLMOps", "u": "evals-ops.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "11.1 Shipping on \"vibe checks\" with no evaluation suite", "u": "evals-ops.html#vibe-checks", "s": "Evaluation & LLMOps", "k": "Quality Reliability No eval suite  no production  The golden dataset and thresholds are how you turn a demo into a decision "}, {"t": "11.2 Only end-to-end evaluation, nothing per component", "u": "evals-ops.html#e2e-only-evals", "s": "Evaluation & LLMOps", "k": "Quality Evaluate the pipeline the way you d test software  unit metrics per stage  integration metrics end to end "}, {"t": "11.3 No tracing of agent trajectories", "u": "evals-ops.html#no-agent-tracing", "s": "Evaluation & LLMOps", "k": "Reliability Compliance If you can t replay an agent s trajectory  you can t debug it  defend it or optimize it  Trace every step with OpenTelemetry "}, {"t": "11.4 LLM-as-judge without calibration", "u": "evals-ops.html#uncalibrated-judge", "s": "Evaluation & LLMOps", "k": "Quality An LLM judge is a classifier  Give it a binary rubric  measure its agreement with experts  and re calibrate it before trusting its numbers "}, {"t": "11.5 No regression gating in CI/CD", "u": "evals-ops.html#no-regression-gate", "s": "Evaluation & LLMOps", "k": "Reliability Quality Every prompt  model and retrieval change is a release  Gate it with evals in CI  canary it  and plan model deprecations ahead "}, {"t": "12. Security, Governance & Responsible AI", "u": "security-gov.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "12.1 No defense against direct and indirect prompt injection", "u": "security-gov.html#prompt-injection", "s": "Security & Governance", "k": "Security Prompt injection is an architecture problem  Never combine private data  untrusted content and an exfiltration channel in one agent context without a break "}, {"t": "12.2 No human in the loop for high-impact actions", "u": "security-gov.html#no-hitl", "s": "Security & Governance", "k": "Compliance Security Autonomy should be proportional to impact  Tier decisions by risk  and give reviewers evidence  not a button "}, {"t": "12.3 No centralized guardrails layer", "u": "security-gov.html#no-guardrail-layer", "s": "Security & Governance", "k": "Security Compliance Scale Safety shouldn t depend on which team built the app  Centralize baseline guardrails in a gateway  as policy as code owned by risk "}, {"t": "12.4 No model risk management or audit trail", "u": "security-gov.html#no-model-risk-mgmt", "s": "Security & Governance", "k": "Compliance Governance that slows delivery gets bypassed  Make it a lightweight  tiered lifecycle fed by automated evidence "}, {"t": "12.5 No cost governance (AI FinOps)", "u": "security-gov.html#no-finops", "s": "Security & Governance", "k": "Cost Every AI call should be attributable to a use case and a business outcome  Cost per resolved ticket is the metric that decides where to scale "}, {"t": "13. Hyperscaler Platforms: AWS, Google Cloud & Microsoft", "u": "hyperscalers.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "13.1 AWS: Bedrock Knowledge Bases with default settings for complex documents", "u": "hyperscalers.html#aws-kb-defaults", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Quality A managed RAG service removes the plumbing  not the design decisions  Configure parsing  chunking  metadata and hybrid retrieval  then evaluate "}, {"t": "13.2 AWS: New agents built on legacy Bedrock Agents with monolithic action groups", "u": "hyperscalers.html#aws-agents-classic", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Reliability Security Scale On AWS  new agent work belongs on AgentCore  any framework  MCP tools through Gateway  and deterministic Cedar policies outside the model "}, {"t": "13.3 AWS: Guardrails on the input only, or not attached at all", "u": "hyperscalers.html#aws-guardrails-partial", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Security Compliance A guardrail that only checks one direction is half a control  Cover input and output  check grounding  version it  and enforce it centrally "}, {"t": "13.4 AWS: Provisioned Throughput bought too early, batch never used", "u": "hyperscalers.html#aws-capacity-misfit", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Pick capacity modes from measured workload shape  on demand for spiky  provisioned for steady  batch for anything that can wait "}, {"t": "13.5 AWS: Cross-region inference enabled without a data-residency review", "u": "hyperscalers.html#aws-cross-region-residency", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Compliance Inference routing is a data residency decision  Map profiles to data classification and enforce it with policy  not convention "}, {"t": "13.6 Google Cloud: Grounding with Google Search for regulated internal answers", "u": "hyperscalers.html#gcp-search-grounding-regulated", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Compliance Quality Choose grounding sources deliberately  Internal policy answers should only ever be grounded in governed internal data "}, {"t": "13.7 Google Cloud: Long-context prompts re-sent without context caching", "u": "hyperscalers.html#gcp-long-context-no-cache", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Latency A large context window without caching re bills the same document on every call  Cache stable prefixes and keep retrieval for pinpoint questions "}, {"t": "13.8 Google Cloud: No VPC Service Controls perimeter around AI services", "u": "hyperscalers.html#gcp-no-vpcsc", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Security Compliance On Google Cloud  IAM decides who can call AI APIs  VPC Service Controls decides from where  and what data can leave  Regulated workloads need both "}, {"t": "13.9 Google Cloud: ADK agents deployed without evaluation in the release path", "u": "hyperscalers.html#gcp-adk-no-evals", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Quality Reliability Easy deployment makes evaluation gates more important  not less  Evaluate agent trajectories in CI before anything reaches the runtime "}, {"t": "13.10 Microsoft: PTU sizing guesses and no 429 handling", "u": "hyperscalers.html#azure-ptu-throttling", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Reliability Size PTUs for the baseline  spill peaks to pay as you go through a gateway  and pick deployment types for residency first  capacity second "}, {"t": "13.11 Microsoft: Quick-start \"chat with your data\" treated as the production RAG platform", "u": "hyperscalers.html#azure-on-your-data", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Quality Scale Wizard generated RAG gets you a demo  Production needs a designed index  hybrid plus semantic ranking  and security trimming tied to Entra ID "}, {"t": "13.12 Microsoft: API keys instead of Entra ID and managed identities", "u": "hyperscalers.html#azure-key-auth", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Security On Azure  every AI caller  app or agent  should be an Entra identity with least privilege RBAC  Disable keys "}, {"t": "13.13 Microsoft: Content filters left at defaults, with no Prompt Shields or custom blocklists", "u": "hyperscalers.html#azure-content-safety-defaults", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Security Compliance Default safety settings are a starting point  Tune filters per domain  shield documents as well as prompts  and measure false positives "}, {"t": "13.14 Cross-cloud: Hard lock-in to one platform's agent SDK", "u": "hyperscalers.html#cloud-sdk-lockin", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Scale Use managed runtimes  but own the seams  Open frameworks  MCP tools and OpenTelemetry keep agents portable across clouds "}, {"t": "13.15 Cross-cloud: Multi-cloud AI just because", "u": "hyperscalers.html#multicloud-by-default", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Compliance Multi cloud should be a justified exception  not a default  Pick a primary platform by data gravity and add others only for named reasons "}, {"t": "13.16 Cross-cloud: Choosing a platform by model benchmarks alone", "u": "hyperscalers.html#platform-by-benchmark", "s": "Hyperscaler Platforms", "k": "Cost Adoption Choose an AI platform for its data  identity and operating fit  Models move every month  your data estate doesn t "}, {"t": "14. Local LLMs vs Hyperscaler Hosting (Cost Management)", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "14.1 Self-hosting \"to save money\" at low or spiky usage", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#selfhost-low-volume", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Cost Self hosting trades variable cost for fixed cost  It only pays off when steady usage is high enough to keep GPUs busy  and people are counted in the TCO "}, {"t": "14.2 Comparing token prices while ignoring operations and quality", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#token-price-only", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Cost Quality The right unit is cost per correct outcome  not cost per token  Price the errors and the operating team before claiming savings "}, {"t": "14.3 Unoptimized self-hosted serving (no batching, quantization or KV cache reuse)", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#inefficient-serving", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Cost Latency If you self host  the serving stack is the cost lever  Continuous batching  prefix caching and validated quantization decide how many GPUs you need "}, {"t": "14.4 All-or-nothing hosting with no hybrid routing", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#no-hybrid-routing", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Cost Quality Local versus cloud is a routing decision  not a religion  Send each request to the cheapest compliant model that meets its quality bar "}, {"t": "14.5 Assuming \"local\" automatically means secure and compliant", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#local-equals-compliant", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Security Compliance On prem isn t automatically secure  Verify the model supply chain  review licenses  patch the stack  and hold local models to the same controls as cloud "}, {"t": "14.6 No caching layer in front of any model", "u": "local-vs-cloud.html#no-cache-layer", "s": "Local LLMs vs Cloud Cost", "k": "Cost Latency The cheapest model call is the one you don t make  Layer prefix  exact and semantic caches  scoped by permissions and invalidated by source changes "}, {"t": "15. Harness Engineering at Every Stage", "u": "harness.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "15.1 Data stage: no golden documents, so parser regressions go unnoticed", "u": "harness.html#harness-data-stage", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Quality Parsing is the first place quality is lost  A golden document harness catches regressions before they spread into every index "}, {"t": "15.2 Retrieval stage: no labelled query set", "u": "harness.html#harness-retrieval-stage", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Quality You can t tune retrieval through the final answer  A labelled query set  with security tests included  makes retrieval an engineering decision "}, {"t": "15.3 Generation stage: no faithfulness harness", "u": "harness.html#harness-generation-stage", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Quality Compliance Evaluate generation with retrieval frozen  Measure claim level faithfulness and refusal behaviour  not how fluent the answer sounds "}, {"t": "15.4 Agent runtime: the prompt treated as the whole agent", "u": "harness.html#harness-runtime-prompt-only", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Reliability Security The model proposes and the harness disposes  Context  tools  sandboxing  hooks and budgets are where agent reliability and safety are actually engineered "}, {"t": "15.5 Agent evaluation: judging only the final answer, not the trajectory", "u": "harness.html#harness-final-answer-only", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Quality Security For agents  how matters as much as what  Evaluate trajectories and safety invariants in simulated multi turn runs "}, {"t": "15.6 Safety: red-teaming once before launch", "u": "harness.html#harness-redteam-once", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Security Red teaming is a regression suite  not an event  Automate attacks  run them on every change  and turn every finding into a permanent test "}, {"t": "15.7 CI/CD: one big eval run instead of a tiered gate", "u": "harness.html#harness-cicd-stage", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Reliability Cost Tier your eval gates like your test pyramid  fast and targeted on every PR  full depth before release  live metrics in canary "}, {"t": "15.8 Production: no online evaluation, drift detection or feedback loop", "u": "harness.html#harness-production-stage", "s": "Harness Engineering", "k": "Quality Reliability Evaluation doesn t end at deployment  Online evals  drift monitors and trace linked feedback keep the harness honest as the world changes "}, {"t": "16. Claude & Other AI Tools", "u": "ai-tools.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "16.1 Claude: Large stable prompts re-sent without prompt caching", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-no-prompt-caching", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Cost Latency With Claude  prompt layout is a cost decision  Put stable content first  set cache breakpoints  and check the cache read tokens "}, {"t": "16.2 Claude: Reasoning effort not matched to the task", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-thinking-misuse", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Cost Latency Quality Reasoning effort is a dial  not a switch  Set it per task from eval results  low for routine work and high for planning and analysis "}, {"t": "16.3 Claude: Procedures pasted into every prompt instead of packaged as Skills", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-skills-ignored", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Cost Quality Scale Institutional know how belongs in versioned  on demand Skills  not pasted into every prompt "}, {"t": "16.4 Claude: One model tier for everything", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-single-tier", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Cost Latency Use the model family as a portfolio  small tiers for volume  the middle tier for most production work  the top tier where reasoning is the bottleneck "}, {"t": "16.5 Claude: Computer use where an API exists, or without a sandbox", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-computer-use-misuse", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Security Reliability Cost Computer use is the integration of last resort  Use APIs and MCP first  and when you do drive a screen  do it in a sandbox with checkpoints "}, {"t": "16.6 Claude: Not choosing deliberately between direct and cloud-marketplace access", "u": "ai-tools.html#claude-access-route", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Compliance Cost Claude is available through several routes  Choose one deliberately on residency  features and governance  and meter them all through one gateway "}, {"t": "16.7 Frameworks: Prompts tuned to one provider's quirks with no portability layer", "u": "ai-tools.html#provider-quirk-prompts", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Reliability Cost Pin behaviour with schemas and evals  not model quirks  so switching models is a measured choice "}, {"t": "16.8 Frameworks: The framework treated as the architecture", "u": "ai-tools.html#framework-as-architecture", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Reliability Cost Pick the lightest abstraction that fits the problem  Frameworks are dependencies to manage  not architectures to adopt "}, {"t": "16.9 Coding agents: Measuring adoption by seats and lines of code", "u": "ai-tools.html#coding-adoption-metrics", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Adoption Cost Measure AI coding tools by delivery outcomes  lead time  change failure rate  rework   not by seats or lines of code "}, {"t": "16.10 Coding agents: Write access to production repos and systems with no guardrails", "u": "ai-tools.html#coding-agent-unguarded", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Security Reliability Give coding agents a sandbox  least privilege credentials  deny rules for destructive commands  and a PR only path to shared branches "}, {"t": "16.11 Coding agents: No repository context or conventions files", "u": "ai-tools.html#coding-no-context-files", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Quality Adoption Coding agents are only as good as the context they start with  Codify conventions in versioned repo context files and shared skills "}, {"t": "16.12 Coding agents: AI-generated code shipped without security and dependency scanning", "u": "ai-tools.html#coding-no-security-scan", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Security AI generated code needs the same or a higher security bar  Scan everything and verify every new dependency "}, {"t": "16.13 Workplace assistants: Enterprise rollout before fixing oversharing", "u": "ai-tools.html#assistant-oversharing", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Security Compliance Workplace assistants respect permissions perfectly  including the wrong ones  Fix oversharing before switching AI on "}, {"t": "16.14 Workplace assistants: Licenses with no enablement, use-case catalog or ROI tracking", "u": "ai-tools.html#assistant-no-enablement", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Adoption Cost A workplace AI rollout is change management  not provisioning  Use persona playbooks  champions  sanctioned advanced paths and measured value "}, {"t": "16.15 Workplace assistants: Banning AI tools with no sanctioned alternative", "u": "ai-tools.html#shadow-ai-ban", "s": "Claude & AI Tools", "k": "Security Adoption Banning AI without a sanctioned alternative creates shadow AI  Make the governed path the easiest one "}, {"t": "17. AI Leadership Anti-Patterns", "u": "leadership.html", "s": "Section"}, {"t": "17.1 Proof-of-concept purgatory", "u": "leadership.html#poc-purgatory", "s": "Leadership Anti-Patterns", "k": "Adoption Cost Run pilots that are designed to graduate  production platform  agreed exit criteria  and a scale or stop decision on a fixed date "}, {"t": "17.2 Technology-first use-case selection", "u": "leadership.html#tech-first-use-cases", "s": "Leadership Anti-Patterns", "k": "Adoption Cost Start from value pools  not technology  Score by value  feasibility and risk  then use the simplest technique that works "}, {"t": "17.3 Every team builds its own AI stack", "u": "leadership.html#no-platform-team", "s": "Leadership Anti-Patterns", "k": "Cost Scale Security Build the paved road once  A shared AI platform turns every new use case into configuration plus domain work  and makes security reviews fast "}, {"t": "17.4 Measuring success by model accuracy instead of business KPIs", "u": "leadership.html#accuracy-not-kpis", "s": "Leadership Anti-Patterns", "k": "Adoption Accuracy is an input  not an outcome  Tie model metrics to business KPIs through a value tree  and redesign the process to capture the value "}, {"t": "17.5 Ignoring change management and workforce impact", "u": "leadership.html#ignoring-change-management", "s": "Leadership Anti-Patterns", "k": "Adoption AI adoption is a people change  Co design  communicate  train  and realign incentives  or the best tool goes unused "}, {"t": "18. Capstone: Enterprise Agentic AI Reference Architecture", "u": "capstone.html", "s": "Section"}];